WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.398 --> 00:00:02.263
- We're going to make a song that

00:00:02.263 --> 00:00:04.656
makes you feel like you
can conquer the world.

00:00:04.656 --> 00:00:06.469
Because I had nothing when I was a kid.

00:00:06.469 --> 00:00:07.381
Nothing.

00:00:07.381 --> 00:00:09.468
I had my first job when I was 13.

00:00:09.468 --> 00:00:12.488
You're not going to see me do things

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:14.039
that are the easy play.

00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:16.542
It's just not going to happen, you know?

00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:19.890
You're going to see me do things
that I have no business doing.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.221
- Hello believe nation,

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.393
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:22.393 --> 00:00:23.657
My one word is believe,

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:25.465
and I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:27.513
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:27.513 --> 00:00:28.960
So to help you on your journey today,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.064
today we're going to learn
from singer songwriter

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.751
producer and entrepreneur Tom DeLonge

00:00:33.751 --> 00:00:37.041
and my take on his top
10 rules of success.

00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:38.725
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.725 --> 00:00:41.196
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:42.988
And as always as you're watching,

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:44.597
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:46.513
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.838
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:48.838 --> 00:00:50.447
Might win a prize as well.

00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:51.839
And also when you write it down,

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:54.561
it's much more likely to
stick to yourself as well.

00:00:54.561 --> 00:00:55.394
Enjoy.

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:02.684
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.527
- When I was in the seventh grade,

00:01:10.527 --> 00:01:12.407
my buddy moved away to Oregon.

00:01:12.407 --> 00:01:16.136
There's a week where I went up
to go visit him in Portland.

00:01:16.136 --> 00:01:18.536
Flew up there by myself, go to his house,

00:01:18.536 --> 00:01:20.181
and the whole thing was is hey,

00:01:20.181 --> 00:01:22.632
he's going to be in school on
some of these days this week,

00:01:22.632 --> 00:01:24.533
so you can shadow him to school,

00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:26.836
or you can hang out 'till
he gets out of school.

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:28.395
I shadowed him one day and I was like,

00:01:28.395 --> 00:01:29.581
this is (censored) weird,

00:01:29.581 --> 00:01:30.675
I'm going to stay at your house and wait.

00:01:30.675 --> 00:01:32.317
But in his room there were

00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:34.229
four things that were very important.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:36.989
A Stiff Little Fingers record.

00:01:36.989 --> 00:01:40.958
There was an All record,
a Dinosaur Junior record,

00:01:40.958 --> 00:01:43.246
and there was an electric guitar.

00:01:43.246 --> 00:01:47.137
So what I did was I would play
those three albums on vinyl

00:01:47.137 --> 00:01:49.514
and I would go over
and pick up this guitar

00:01:49.514 --> 00:01:51.355
to keep myself entertained
throughout the day

00:01:51.355 --> 00:01:53.758
for those three or four days
that he would be at school,

00:01:53.758 --> 00:01:55.148
and so he'd come back and we'd

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.537
skate at night and do whatever.

00:01:56.537 --> 00:01:58.711
That changed my life.

00:01:58.711 --> 00:02:00.926
You know, it changed my life.

00:02:00.926 --> 00:02:05.093
Hearing punk rock music
and picking up the guitar,

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:10.179
something clicked where it's almost like

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:11.532
there's a bunch of gasoline on the ground

00:02:11.532 --> 00:02:14.974
and someone flicks a
cigarette and it just ignites.

00:02:14.974 --> 00:02:17.548
It was a chemical reaction that just

00:02:17.548 --> 00:02:21.053
flipped on a switch
that nothing could take

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:23.926
that guitar out of my hand
from that point forward.

00:02:23.926 --> 00:02:25.985
Being in a band, you have to
constantly reinvent yourself.

00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:28.571
You have to constantly be reminded

00:02:28.571 --> 00:02:31.219
how there's someone better than you.

00:02:31.219 --> 00:02:32.886
Every other weekend.

00:02:34.032 --> 00:02:36.214
And you're constantly reminded

00:02:36.214 --> 00:02:39.009
how you would have
nothing without your fans.

00:02:39.009 --> 00:02:41.862
So you have to take into
account what they're thinking

00:02:41.862 --> 00:02:43.675
and what they're interested in.

00:02:43.675 --> 00:02:45.247
And all those things matter.

00:02:45.247 --> 00:02:47.111
But it doesn't matter what art you do.

00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:48.476
Doesn't matter if it's making an album

00:02:48.476 --> 00:02:49.815
or a movie or a book or whatever.

00:02:49.815 --> 00:02:52.102
Thinking about all those types of things,

00:02:52.102 --> 00:02:54.113
the hard work involved.

00:02:54.113 --> 00:02:57.363
If you want to have like a casual life,

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:00.733
you don't want to be in a band.

00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:03.522
You never sleep, it's
really really hard work.

00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:05.252
You're exhausted all the time.

00:03:05.252 --> 00:03:07.312
You're almost always sick and you're

00:03:07.312 --> 00:03:09.546
fighting with your business
partners all day long.

00:03:09.546 --> 00:03:11.213
It's just how it is.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.520
But I have a very very strong work ethic

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.859
because I had nothing when I was a kid.

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:17.775
Nothing.

00:03:17.775 --> 00:03:19.786
I had my first job when I was 13.

00:03:19.786 --> 00:03:23.036
I had two jobs when I was 16, you know?

00:03:23.998 --> 00:03:26.113
But I think a lot of it's probably

00:03:26.113 --> 00:03:27.832
because I'm extraordinarily fearful

00:03:27.832 --> 00:03:31.306
about having to do what
someone tells me to do,

00:03:31.306 --> 00:03:33.609
so I'd rather be doing everything.

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:35.987
Because at least no
one told me to do that.

00:03:35.987 --> 00:03:37.508
(host laughing)
You know, I don't know.

00:03:37.508 --> 00:03:39.934
I think when you grow up as a kid,

00:03:39.934 --> 00:03:42.368
you know you're always
looking for your voice.

00:03:42.368 --> 00:03:44.728
You're always looking
for your independence.

00:03:44.728 --> 00:03:47.837
You're always looking
to find out who you are.

00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:49.409
With skateboarding,

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:51.421
it was a means of transportation
first and foremost.

00:03:51.421 --> 00:03:53.469
As a kid if you have a
skateboard you can get out

00:03:53.469 --> 00:03:54.550
and you can go somewhere.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.663
You can't drive yet, you know.

00:03:55.663 --> 00:03:57.110
So it's kind of like this is your tool,

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:58.625
your vehicle to leave.

00:03:58.625 --> 00:04:00.234
And then you're kind of goin'
okay well it's my freedom,

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.319
but expanded from just transportation.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:03.364
It's something you do on your own,

00:04:03.364 --> 00:04:04.842
you don't need a whole
team to play it with you.

00:04:04.842 --> 00:04:06.159
You just do it with--

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:07.475
And you can go skate by yourself.

00:04:07.475 --> 00:04:09.706
And I think that music did a
lot of the same kind of stuff.

00:04:09.706 --> 00:04:12.632
I think that when you're joining
a band for the first time,

00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:13.860
you're trying to find your voice.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.302
You're trying to find that freedom,

00:04:15.302 --> 00:04:16.909
to get in a van and get out of town.

00:04:16.909 --> 00:04:18.466
You're trying to figure out who you are

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:20.093
by defining it through your music,

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:22.064
the way you dress and
who you hang out with.

00:04:22.064 --> 00:04:22.964
It was funny.

00:04:22.964 --> 00:04:24.319
Bono once said that they didn't even know

00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:26.456
to start the band U2
until they saw the Ramones

00:04:26.456 --> 00:04:28.671
and realized you don't have to shred at

00:04:28.671 --> 00:04:32.254
your instruments to be
in a band, you know?

00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:35.709
You can get the emotion
across in a different way.

00:04:35.709 --> 00:04:37.339
It was kind of more what you were saying

00:04:37.339 --> 00:04:38.580
and how you were living,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.380
not as much as your solos,
you know, on guitar.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.580
So what I learned from
being in a punk rock band

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.570
was really these ethics
of getting out there

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.115
and doing things yourself.

00:04:49.115 --> 00:04:50.813
I've always been fascinated with space.

00:04:50.813 --> 00:04:52.531
I'd always tell people this,

00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:54.491
'cause it's one of my favorite quotes.

00:04:54.491 --> 00:04:57.246
If space is infinite, like there's no end,

00:04:57.246 --> 00:04:59.574
there's no (censored) end to space,

00:04:59.574 --> 00:05:03.064
then it's infinite the
possibility, you know?

00:05:03.064 --> 00:05:04.947
Anything you think of right now

00:05:04.947 --> 00:05:07.912
could be happening because it's that big.

00:05:07.912 --> 00:05:10.779
And that was always really exciting to me,

00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:13.907
'cause I'm kind of
wired to be an optimist,

00:05:13.907 --> 00:05:15.723
and when I started playing guitar

00:05:15.723 --> 00:05:19.022
with these types of
rhythmic trance-like echoes,

00:05:19.022 --> 00:05:21.378
I started to feel that way.

00:05:21.378 --> 00:05:23.060
I started to feel like I
was floating through space.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.751
First thing I did was I set
up the studio in my house,

00:05:26.751 --> 00:05:28.838
so I could be at home, for the first time.

00:05:28.838 --> 00:05:30.651
And then I put up these
big television screens,

00:05:30.651 --> 00:05:34.321
and I was playing all
science fiction films.

00:05:34.321 --> 00:05:36.759
Like Blade Runner or 2001,

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:40.225
anything that was visually stunning.

00:05:40.225 --> 00:05:42.316
But then I also had these
Steven Ambrose books,

00:05:42.316 --> 00:05:45.134
these big coffee table books
with World War 2 pictures.

00:05:45.134 --> 00:05:47.636
And I kept going over there
looking at the space movie

00:05:47.636 --> 00:05:50.707
and then lookin' back down
at the World War 2 photos,

00:05:50.707 --> 00:05:52.133
and back to the space
movie, and I was like, wow.

00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:55.560
That juxtaposition is gnarly, you know?

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.564
Here's the Earth horizon and the

00:05:57.564 --> 00:05:59.210
sun coming up and it's so beautiful,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.862
and then here's guys in a
ditch, bodies everywhere.

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:04.062
And I'm kind of going,
is that the same place?

00:06:04.062 --> 00:06:05.979
(laughing) You know, Earth
does both those things?

00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:08.759
And I said, what it would it sound like

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:12.217
with marching coming into the song or

00:06:12.217 --> 00:06:13.717
what would it sound
like if it sounded like

00:06:13.717 --> 00:06:17.159
a battalion of tanks rolling
in as the song starts?

00:06:17.159 --> 00:06:18.159
And Critter,

00:06:19.824 --> 00:06:24.469
which was my engineer and
co-pilot in all of this stuff.

00:06:24.469 --> 00:06:27.333
He pointed to a file cabinet.

00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:28.305
And he's all,

00:06:28.305 --> 00:06:30.598
just slam that file cabinet
shut, open and shut.

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:32.411
And I was like, all right.

00:06:32.411 --> 00:06:33.722
And so he grabs a mic and does it.

00:06:33.722 --> 00:06:37.050
And it's just. (creaking and slamming)

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.659
And you just hear the
mechanics and this kind of

00:06:38.659 --> 00:06:42.076
grinding-like metallic noise with a slam.

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:46.632
And he put it in there and
he did his thing to it,

00:06:46.632 --> 00:06:47.912
pitched it down.

00:06:47.912 --> 00:06:49.725
The next thing you know, when
it's happening in a rhythm,

00:06:49.725 --> 00:06:53.398
it just sounded like a million
troops stomping on your face.

00:06:53.398 --> 00:06:55.345
And I was like, oh my God.

00:06:55.345 --> 00:06:57.987
I've never heard something
like that in a song.

00:06:57.987 --> 00:07:01.312
And next thing you know we're
doing that on all the songs.

00:07:01.312 --> 00:07:03.746
We found something that was unique to do,

00:07:03.746 --> 00:07:07.368
which was, you know, it's sound design.

00:07:07.368 --> 00:07:09.709
You see it in movies all
the time, it's sound design.

00:07:09.709 --> 00:07:12.009
But sound design crafted into a song.

00:07:12.009 --> 00:07:14.759
And four songs into that writing,

00:07:16.812 --> 00:07:19.101
we wrote a song called The Adventure.

00:07:19.101 --> 00:07:23.268
And that just, that was a
life-changing moment for me.

00:07:28.142 --> 00:07:30.315
We put some rocks in a shoebox,

00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:33.199
and we rolled the shoebox
around with all these pebbles.

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:36.585
(grinding) And then we grabbed that,

00:07:36.585 --> 00:07:39.166
and created a rhythm out of it.

00:07:39.166 --> 00:07:41.835
And so when we added some stomps

00:07:41.835 --> 00:07:44.087
and this rolling box of rocks,

00:07:44.087 --> 00:07:46.976
it created this grind-y kind of rhythm,

00:07:46.976 --> 00:07:50.905
and then we added a riff to it
that was a very simple riff.

00:07:50.905 --> 00:07:52.661
Kind of sad sounding.

00:07:52.661 --> 00:07:54.050
But I didn't know what I was

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.625
going to play for the main song.

00:07:55.625 --> 00:07:58.293
So we started with the
rhythm, then put a riff,

00:07:58.293 --> 00:07:59.996
then I was like, okay so
where does it go from here?

00:07:59.996 --> 00:08:02.829
So I stomped on the pedal in time,

00:08:03.705 --> 00:08:07.378
and I was for hours straight
sitting at the computer,

00:08:07.378 --> 00:08:09.045
I think there's footage of this,

00:08:09.045 --> 00:08:11.368
of the documentary we made.

00:08:11.368 --> 00:08:13.815
Maybe you could show it right here.

00:08:13.815 --> 00:08:17.982
(music playing)
Where does this need to go?

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.014
I could bring in some
rhythm underneath that.

00:08:24.014 --> 00:08:25.504
I think that it's what happens right here

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:27.097
is what's throwing me for a loop.

00:08:27.097 --> 00:08:27.930
- Right.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:30.608
- I was sitting at the computer,

00:08:30.608 --> 00:08:33.691
and it just kind of starts to happen.

00:08:34.667 --> 00:08:37.917
(rapid guitar playing)

00:08:47.065 --> 00:08:49.065
Here, it's a little dry.

00:08:50.299 --> 00:08:52.783
On the song, in the moment,

00:08:52.783 --> 00:08:54.638
it was something that gave you

00:08:54.638 --> 00:08:57.129
the chills up and down your arms.

00:08:57.129 --> 00:08:59.159
And I remember stopping
and looking at Critter,

00:08:59.159 --> 00:09:01.939
and I was like holy
(censored), holy (censored).

00:09:01.939 --> 00:09:03.394
We got somethin'.

00:09:03.394 --> 00:09:07.561
("The Adventure" by Angels and Airwaves)

00:09:14.637 --> 00:09:16.429
- I remember during writing
the words, actually,

00:09:16.429 --> 00:09:19.379
that friend who's wife left him

00:09:19.379 --> 00:09:22.228
just figured that she could
find something better.

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:23.121
And he loved her.

00:09:23.121 --> 00:09:24.166
I remember him saying,

00:09:24.166 --> 00:09:25.337
I don't know if you'll
ever know what it's like

00:09:25.337 --> 00:09:28.241
to love somebody so much more
than they will ever love you,

00:09:28.241 --> 00:09:29.741
is what he said to me.

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:32.630
So I remember feeling so beat up

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.709
from everything I was going through

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:36.506
with the whole Blink
situation and trying to

00:09:36.506 --> 00:09:39.469
find a new identity and
all that kind of stuff,

00:09:39.469 --> 00:09:41.367
and then my friend says that to me.

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:43.293
And I was like, well (censored) then,

00:09:43.293 --> 00:09:45.976
we're going to make a song that makes you

00:09:45.976 --> 00:09:48.574
feel like you can conquer the world.

00:09:48.574 --> 00:09:50.824
That night after I sang it,

00:09:52.022 --> 00:09:54.730
I remember sitting in the studio at 2 a.m.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.168
It was at my house, my family's asleep.

00:09:57.168 --> 00:09:59.012
And I was listening to this thing,

00:09:59.012 --> 00:10:00.131
and about halfway through the song

00:10:00.131 --> 00:10:01.886
I have these tears in
my eyes, and I was just,

00:10:01.886 --> 00:10:04.242
I felt like there was
something magical happening.

00:10:04.242 --> 00:10:05.759
That happens to me every once in a while

00:10:05.759 --> 00:10:07.899
where I'm apart of something artistically

00:10:07.899 --> 00:10:09.832
where I get kind of
overwhelmed emotionally

00:10:09.832 --> 00:10:12.836
and feel like I'm really on
to something that's important.

00:10:12.836 --> 00:10:14.904
Not that I think it's like great

00:10:14.904 --> 00:10:16.476
or better than anything else

00:10:16.476 --> 00:10:19.512
or that's it's going to
be something giant or big,

00:10:19.512 --> 00:10:20.610
it's because I feel like it touches on

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.601
something that's important to me.

00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:26.021
And it like grabbed a hold of like nerve

00:10:26.021 --> 00:10:28.217
and just gripping it and I can feel it,

00:10:28.217 --> 00:10:30.903
and it's like electric
waves through your body.

00:10:30.903 --> 00:10:32.324
It doesn't happen on every single thing,

00:10:32.324 --> 00:10:34.178
but it happens every so often.

00:10:34.178 --> 00:10:37.815
And you kind of go wow,
this what I was meant to do,

00:10:37.815 --> 00:10:40.082
was this one little thing, you know?

00:10:40.082 --> 00:10:42.773
And that song was one of them.

00:10:42.773 --> 00:10:45.312
- I think I learned over
the past handful of years

00:10:45.312 --> 00:10:48.562
that what I really enjoyed about a band

00:10:49.755 --> 00:10:51.806
wasn't being on stage,

00:10:51.806 --> 00:10:55.291
it was just the idea
that I'm involved somehow

00:10:55.291 --> 00:10:57.929
in leading a group of people to

00:10:57.929 --> 00:10:59.449
feel something at the same time,

00:10:59.449 --> 00:11:00.562
I thought that was really cool.

00:11:00.562 --> 00:11:01.873
That's what I kind of dissected.

00:11:01.873 --> 00:11:03.488
'Cause I was doing a lot of things.

00:11:03.488 --> 00:11:06.178
I was equally interested in a lot of

00:11:06.178 --> 00:11:09.178
different things besides just music.

00:11:10.035 --> 00:11:12.265
And so a lot of people are wondering,

00:11:12.265 --> 00:11:13.526
why would you not want to be on

00:11:13.526 --> 00:11:15.614
a big stage doing all this stuff?

00:11:15.614 --> 00:11:18.811
And I just, God, do I Instagram this?

00:11:18.811 --> 00:11:20.149
Am I supposed to go on
Instagram and tell you

00:11:20.149 --> 00:11:22.395
all these things that I feel about--

00:11:22.395 --> 00:11:23.895
You know, I don't.

00:11:25.195 --> 00:11:26.622
But really what it boils down to

00:11:26.622 --> 00:11:30.404
is I have a pretty wild imagination,

00:11:30.404 --> 00:11:35.207
and I have a few set of
skills that I've developed

00:11:35.207 --> 00:11:37.786
not only inspiring
artists or leading people

00:11:37.786 --> 00:11:39.948
or putting business models to things

00:11:39.948 --> 00:11:42.948
or being entrepreneurial
and also understanding,

00:11:42.948 --> 00:11:45.249
I'm not really good at any one thing,

00:11:45.249 --> 00:11:47.959
but I'm decently good at a
bunch of different things.

00:11:47.959 --> 00:11:49.991
I kind of figure, I said,

00:11:49.991 --> 00:11:52.493
it might be a really good place for me

00:11:52.493 --> 00:11:54.703
to sit in the middle of a bunch of artists

00:11:54.703 --> 00:11:58.371
that come together that can do something

00:11:58.371 --> 00:11:59.677
a bit more revolutionary than

00:11:59.677 --> 00:12:03.100
each artist doing one thing on their own.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.477
That's where that came from.

00:12:05.477 --> 00:12:08.477
(steady rock music)

00:12:11.124 --> 00:12:13.882
- Lou's Records is a very important place.

00:12:13.882 --> 00:12:18.182
It was the most famous
record store in San Diego.

00:12:18.182 --> 00:12:21.620
And one of the most famous in the country.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:22.701
When Blink first started,

00:12:22.701 --> 00:12:25.749
I drove around about a 20
mile radius from this store,

00:12:25.749 --> 00:12:28.736
and I broke into every
high school at night,

00:12:28.736 --> 00:12:31.698
and I made Blink flyers and I put them

00:12:31.698 --> 00:12:33.981
in every single locker
at all these high schools

00:12:33.981 --> 00:12:37.236
that were playing at the club downtown.

00:12:37.236 --> 00:12:38.622
At that point in time,

00:12:38.622 --> 00:12:41.936
everyone in San Diego wanted
to support their local bands.

00:12:41.936 --> 00:12:43.599
It was just a really big scene.

00:12:43.599 --> 00:12:47.394
So because I put those
flyers in those lockers,

00:12:47.394 --> 00:12:51.938
we ended up having our
first show at a big club,

00:12:51.938 --> 00:12:53.456
all the people came,

00:12:53.456 --> 00:12:55.081
and we had enough people that watched us

00:12:55.081 --> 00:12:56.784
that the guy that owned the club

00:12:56.784 --> 00:13:00.407
invited us the following week
to play on the big stage.

00:13:00.407 --> 00:13:02.690
So what we did was not
only played that show,

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.140
we rushed in, made a
really quick recording,

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:06.221
and it was our first recording

00:13:06.221 --> 00:13:07.846
with a little Buddha statue on it,

00:13:07.846 --> 00:13:10.403
people commonly refer to
that record as Buddha.

00:13:10.403 --> 00:13:13.322
We made that to sell here in this store.

00:13:13.322 --> 00:13:17.062
So we would put five tapes in at a time,

00:13:17.062 --> 00:13:18.635
it was in a time where tapes were selling.

00:13:18.635 --> 00:13:20.631
And every week we would call up,

00:13:20.631 --> 00:13:21.947
and there like, they're gone.

00:13:21.947 --> 00:13:23.447
And we're like oh my God, so
we put five more, they're gone.

00:13:23.447 --> 00:13:25.165
So then we gave them 10, they're gone.

00:13:25.165 --> 00:13:27.229
Them we gave them 20, next thing you know,

00:13:27.229 --> 00:13:29.408
we've sold thousands
of these tape cassettes

00:13:29.408 --> 00:13:32.773
out of Lou's Records, and
then we got our start.

00:13:32.773 --> 00:13:34.928
It was, you know, little suburban kids.

00:13:34.928 --> 00:13:37.929
We had a dream to be in
the punk rock section,

00:13:37.929 --> 00:13:42.096
and I'm here now looking at
all these things I grew up on,

00:13:42.973 --> 00:13:46.502
and it was so insanely important to me.

00:13:46.502 --> 00:13:48.352
My inspiration usually comes from,

00:13:48.352 --> 00:13:52.192
once the music for the
song is more or less

00:13:52.192 --> 00:13:53.890
close to being completed,

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.701
I'll just try and find an emotional

00:13:55.701 --> 00:13:57.401
current in it that makes sense.

00:13:57.401 --> 00:13:59.593
I'll either identify with is
and write exactly like it,

00:13:59.593 --> 00:14:02.255
or try to write the opposite.

00:14:02.255 --> 00:14:03.764
It's a good question,

00:14:03.764 --> 00:14:04.971
because I think it's
different for every artist.

00:14:04.971 --> 00:14:08.394
You know they always say, you
know, I write the words first.

00:14:08.394 --> 00:14:10.676
But not for myself, I like
to write the music first,

00:14:10.676 --> 00:14:13.017
'cause that to me is
like the foundation of

00:14:13.017 --> 00:14:15.613
the building you build on top of it.

00:14:15.613 --> 00:14:20.111
- How much have you changed
as a creative person?

00:14:20.111 --> 00:14:21.156
- Oh a lot.

00:14:21.156 --> 00:14:22.656
Well I'm learning so much.

00:14:22.656 --> 00:14:24.989
I mean I'm learning so much.

00:14:27.362 --> 00:14:29.374
When I come up with a good
idea, I stick with it.

00:14:29.374 --> 00:14:31.073
That's the difference.

00:14:31.073 --> 00:14:33.120
I can come up with a lot
of ideas, but anyone can.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.189
When I come up with something, you know,

00:14:35.189 --> 00:14:36.895
this is something that's capable of

00:14:36.895 --> 00:14:39.723
me growing and I still will like this.

00:14:39.723 --> 00:14:40.963
You know? (laughing)

00:14:40.963 --> 00:14:42.751
This is something that's big
enough and ambitious enough

00:14:42.751 --> 00:14:44.608
that in four years from now,

00:14:44.608 --> 00:14:46.801
if I really want to
become a space engineer

00:14:46.801 --> 00:14:48.020
and build space ships,

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.910
I could probably put those
space ships in the movie

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:51.317
or in the book or something, you know?

00:14:51.317 --> 00:14:52.362
That's kind of how I think.

00:14:52.362 --> 00:14:55.851
Come up with projects that
can last long time and grow,

00:14:55.851 --> 00:14:56.985
and then as I grow,

00:14:56.985 --> 00:15:00.457
I'm still interested in what I've created.

00:15:00.457 --> 00:15:02.650
So yeah, but I plan for that.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:05.381
I plan for my evolving ideas.

00:15:05.381 --> 00:15:09.064
I think my biggest issue is I don't

00:15:09.064 --> 00:15:12.805
actively go out and search
for admiration from people.

00:15:12.805 --> 00:15:15.563
Or to explain what I'm thinking to people.

00:15:15.563 --> 00:15:18.435
Or to validate my actions.

00:15:18.435 --> 00:15:21.472
It's the first rule in politics,

00:15:21.472 --> 00:15:24.853
you're suppose to define
the other candidate for them

00:15:24.853 --> 00:15:26.519
before they can define themselves.

00:15:26.519 --> 00:15:29.351
And what happens to me is that the fans,

00:15:29.351 --> 00:15:31.982
the good and the bad and
the good and the bad press,

00:15:31.982 --> 00:15:36.385
they have defined before
I go out there and do it.

00:15:36.385 --> 00:15:38.615
And what's happened is they've
defined me in such a way,

00:15:38.615 --> 00:15:42.282
probably in many places,
that is inaccurate.

00:15:43.345 --> 00:15:45.477
You know, I don't think people know that

00:15:45.477 --> 00:15:50.455
I listen to scholars every
single night before I go to bed.

00:15:50.455 --> 00:15:52.522
I put it on my night stand
and listen to lectures,

00:15:52.522 --> 00:15:55.373
I don't think people know
that I read books on physics.

00:15:55.373 --> 00:15:57.677
I don't think people know that I'm

00:15:57.677 --> 00:16:01.207
totally still by fascinated
by (censored) jokes (laughs)

00:16:01.207 --> 00:16:03.422
even though I'm doing some
of those things, you know?

00:16:03.422 --> 00:16:06.217
I don't think people know
that my favorite thing

00:16:06.217 --> 00:16:09.252
in the world outside
of being with my family

00:16:09.252 --> 00:16:12.380
is sitting with my Great
Dane and my German Shepherd

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.466
at dog beach, you know?

00:16:14.466 --> 00:16:16.633
And looking at the sunset.

00:16:17.707 --> 00:16:18.942
I don't think people know that

00:16:18.942 --> 00:16:22.235
I'm still completely
fascinated with skateboarding.

00:16:22.235 --> 00:16:24.341
And my son is so into baseball

00:16:24.341 --> 00:16:27.486
that my way of relating to
both is relating Nike SB,

00:16:27.486 --> 00:16:28.982
which is Nike Skateboarding.

00:16:28.982 --> 00:16:30.375
I don't think people know that's why

00:16:30.375 --> 00:16:32.971
I'm wearing Nike in
certain Instagram posts.

00:16:32.971 --> 00:16:35.620
Because it's a way for me
to connect with my boy,

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:37.177
but also still have a foot in the

00:16:37.177 --> 00:16:39.189
skateboarding world from which I came.

00:16:39.189 --> 00:16:44.098
I don't think people know
that I'm completely immersed

00:16:44.098 --> 00:16:46.978
in trying to understand ancient religions,

00:16:46.978 --> 00:16:49.441
and you know why we are the way we are,

00:16:49.441 --> 00:16:51.492
and why we're warring with each other.

00:16:51.492 --> 00:16:53.448
And they probably don't know that I think

00:16:53.448 --> 00:16:56.429
I a (censored) solution. (laughs)

00:16:56.429 --> 00:16:57.527
I'm one of those guys that likes to

00:16:57.527 --> 00:16:58.988
look at the biggest ever thing and go,

00:16:58.988 --> 00:17:00.400
I bet you I have way to fix it!

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.171
Which is the biggest joke ever,

00:17:02.171 --> 00:17:03.984
but that's how my brain thinks.

00:17:03.984 --> 00:17:07.605
The only thing that takes me away from

00:17:07.605 --> 00:17:10.974
my maddening thoughts is
if I'm learning something.

00:17:10.974 --> 00:17:12.509
For me what I do is I learn something

00:17:12.509 --> 00:17:14.754
and how do I turn that
into an art project?

00:17:14.754 --> 00:17:16.310
So it's a non-fiction book or it's

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:17.829
a documentary or it's a lecture,

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:20.556
and what does that look
like as an animation?

00:17:20.556 --> 00:17:21.873
What does that look like as a song?

00:17:21.873 --> 00:17:24.659
What does that look
like as a feature film?

00:17:24.659 --> 00:17:26.127
What does that look like as a story

00:17:26.127 --> 00:17:27.797
that has all those things in it?

00:17:27.797 --> 00:17:28.907
That's a challenge.

00:17:28.907 --> 00:17:31.490
And that keeps my mind at ease.

00:17:33.738 --> 00:17:36.776
I used to tell Mark in
Blink that I admired him

00:17:36.776 --> 00:17:40.359
because he was always
satisfied with Blink.

00:17:42.115 --> 00:17:45.984
It satisfied him, that it
was an incredible thing,

00:17:45.984 --> 00:17:48.093
and we were given such
an incredible thing,

00:17:48.093 --> 00:17:49.704
and we were so lucky to have it,

00:17:49.704 --> 00:17:50.999
and all these different things.

00:17:50.999 --> 00:17:52.520
And I agreed with all those things,

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:55.315
but for some reason it's
not stopping my desire

00:17:55.315 --> 00:17:58.570
to go out and learn more and try more

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:01.751
and push myself more and be surrounded by

00:18:01.751 --> 00:18:06.339
other people who I find
are completely fascinating.

00:18:06.339 --> 00:18:09.893
You know, you're not
going to see me do things

00:18:09.893 --> 00:18:11.976
that are the easy play.

00:18:11.976 --> 00:18:14.332
It's just not going to happen, you know?

00:18:14.332 --> 00:18:15.666
You're going to see me do things that

00:18:15.666 --> 00:18:17.790
I have no business doing.

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:20.789
And sometimes it's music,
sometimes it's novels,

00:18:20.789 --> 00:18:24.372
sometimes it's feature
films or animations,

00:18:25.835 --> 00:18:29.003
sometimes it's all those things.

00:18:29.003 --> 00:18:31.310
But at the end of the day I'm still just

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:33.736
a very normal kid from a broken family

00:18:33.736 --> 00:18:35.726
in a shitty little suburban town,

00:18:35.726 --> 00:18:37.847
and that should just prove to everybody

00:18:37.847 --> 00:18:39.368
that they can do it too.

00:18:39.368 --> 00:18:43.991
("What's My Age Again" by Blink 182)

00:18:43.991 --> 00:18:45.491
- When it comes to tattooing,

00:18:45.491 --> 00:18:47.410
there's definitely been a learning curve.

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:50.743
I didn't start out a master of my craft.

00:18:54.081 --> 00:18:56.331
(laughter)

00:19:03.907 --> 00:19:05.605
Between a tattoo artist and his client,

00:19:05.605 --> 00:19:07.855
communication is essential.

00:19:08.951 --> 00:19:12.846
All right man, it's
all done, check it out.

00:19:12.846 --> 00:19:14.781
- I wanted a ship!

00:19:14.781 --> 00:19:16.338
- Oh, with a P.

00:19:16.338 --> 00:19:18.299
This is with a T.

00:19:18.299 --> 00:19:21.254
This is (censored), it's more you.

00:19:21.254 --> 00:19:22.695
Some people compare me to Monet,

00:19:22.695 --> 00:19:24.560
some to Manet, some to Picasso.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:28.393
But I think I've developed
a style all my own.

00:19:29.489 --> 00:19:34.163
All right, tell me what
you think of this one here.

00:19:34.163 --> 00:19:36.906
- What the (censored)?

00:19:36.906 --> 00:19:41.112
(tattoo gun buzzing)
(groaning in pain)

00:19:41.112 --> 00:19:43.645
- Art is a journey, not a destination.

00:19:43.645 --> 00:19:46.120
I'd never want to be perfect,

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:48.801
that's a dead end street.

00:19:48.801 --> 00:19:50.065
All right we're all done,

00:19:50.065 --> 00:19:51.605
take a look at that,
tell me what you think.

00:19:51.605 --> 00:19:52.605
- Binkl 182?

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.830
I'll see you in hell!

00:19:58.487 --> 00:20:01.487
(audience cheering)

00:20:03.933 --> 00:20:05.307
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:05.307 --> 00:20:08.376
I made this video because
Hyperspree asked me to.

00:20:08.376 --> 00:20:10.001
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:20:10.001 --> 00:20:11.232
that you want me to profile next,

00:20:11.232 --> 00:20:12.381
check out the link in the description

00:20:12.381 --> 00:20:14.450
and you can go and cast your vote.

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:15.709
I'd also love to know which clip

00:20:15.709 --> 00:20:17.318
resonated the most with you,

00:20:17.318 --> 00:20:18.945
what lesson are you going
to take from this video,

00:20:18.945 --> 00:20:20.972
and immediately apply to your life

00:20:20.972 --> 00:20:22.752
or to your business somehow.

00:20:22.752 --> 00:20:23.828
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:20:23.828 --> 00:20:26.127
I'm really curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:20:26.127 --> 00:20:27.396
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:20:27.396 --> 00:20:29.370
to Jason Fonceca and Yuki.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:30.850
Thank you guys so much for picking up

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:32.421
a copy of my book at the bookstore

00:20:32.421 --> 00:20:35.113
and posting that picture
to your social channels.

00:20:35.113 --> 00:20:36.774
I really appreciate the support,

00:20:36.774 --> 00:20:39.407
and I'm really glad that you
enjoyed the read through.

00:20:39.407 --> 00:20:40.559
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:40.559 --> 00:20:42.093
I believe in you,

00:20:42.093 --> 00:20:44.075
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:20:44.075 --> 00:20:47.217
And whatever your one word is, much love.

00:20:47.217 --> 00:20:48.717
I'll see you soon.

00:21:01.723 --> 00:21:04.186
- You should follow
your passions, you know?

00:21:04.186 --> 00:21:08.353
And you should make sure
you do something you love.

00:21:09.844 --> 00:21:11.451
That's all I've learned,

00:21:11.451 --> 00:21:12.986
is that if you're doing
something you love,

00:21:12.986 --> 00:21:15.303
you'll work harder at
it and make it happen.

00:21:15.303 --> 00:21:16.914
Promise you that.

00:21:16.914 --> 00:21:18.436
I actually take feedback really well,

00:21:18.436 --> 00:21:21.122
but I think it has to be from--

00:21:21.122 --> 00:21:22.898
You have to be with people you respect,

00:21:22.898 --> 00:21:24.779
in that you really genuinely think

00:21:24.779 --> 00:21:27.370
that they make good decisions, you know?

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:29.627
It's so much easier when
you can trust the person.

00:21:29.627 --> 00:21:32.736
When that person looks
me in the eye and says,

00:21:32.736 --> 00:21:34.747
it kind of sucks what you just did.

00:21:34.747 --> 00:21:36.148
You go, (censored) he means it,

00:21:36.148 --> 00:21:38.262
and you know I agree with him.

00:21:38.262 --> 00:21:39.410
You know?

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:41.203
And that's where it works.

00:21:41.203 --> 00:21:42.833
So you have to, you know,

00:21:42.833 --> 00:21:45.040
you have to get people
that you really respect,

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:46.867
that you've built up the trust,

00:21:46.867 --> 00:21:49.468
and you empower each other and you can

00:21:49.468 --> 00:21:52.269
really really get it done.

00:21:52.269 --> 00:21:55.102
And strong debate is a part of it.

00:21:55.938 --> 00:22:00.105
If you have intelligent
individuals, it's not hard.

